<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<services version="1.0">

  <container id="default" version="1.0">
    <search />
    <document-api />
    <!-- This package SHIPS WITHOUT an <accesslog> element on purpose (if you
         are reading a deployed copy that has one, it was injected below):
         Vespa's default (JSON, one line
         per HTTP request, hourly rotation, zstd) is what most deployments
         want, and declaring it explicitly would oblige us to restate
         fileNamePattern and friends — Vespa rejects a configured access log
         without one, so an explicit element means owning defaults we would
         rather inherit.

         It is not free, and the cost scales with the store's OWN traffic
         rather than user traffic: the write path is read-heavy (dedup
         existence checks, guard probes, supersession searches), so a
         mirroring deployment logs mostly itself. Measured 2026-08-07 on a
         router ingesting ~10-30k ev/s: ~500 req/s and ~3.2 GB/hour, rotated
         and compressed but never pruned — enough to evict a Kubernetes pod
         off an emptyDir sizeLimit roughly hourly.

         Set VESPA_ACCESS_LOG=disabled and VespaApp.zipBytes() injects a
         disabled accesslog element here at deploy time. Right where the HTTP
         client is your own ingest and the useful record is the router's stats
         plus Vespa's metrics. -->
    <nodes>
      <!-- The container JVM's DIRECT (off-heap) buffer budget: bounded first,
           raised second. This is the process serving :8080 — every query AND
           every feed operation — and Vespa runs it with
           -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError, so ONE thread failing to reserve a
           buffer kills the endpoint outright. Vespa derives the ceiling from
           the heap: 208 MiB with its default 1536m.

           Measured on staging 2026-08-14 (~211M events, ~500 req/s of the
           store's OWN dedup/guard/supersession reads): the container died 8
           times in 18 minutes on `Cannot reserve … bytes of direct buffer
           memory`, with the pool sitting at 93-98% full and seven of the eight
           failures asking for only 8-16 MiB. Steady-state exhaustion, not one
           pathological allocation. It exits 1, so Kubernetes sees a healthy pod
           while :8080 is down behind the sentinel's exponential restart penalty
           (2s -> 126s).

           The saturation is CACHING, not demand. jrt reads into HEAP buffers
           (com.yahoo.jrt.Buffer calls ByteBuffer.allocate), so every socket read
           borrows a temporary DIRECT buffer from sun.nio.ch.Util, sized to the
           destination's remaining capacity. That cache is per thread, holds up
           to IOV_MAX (1024 on Linux) buffers, and — with
           jdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize unset, i.e. Long.MAX_VALUE — retains each
           one at its HIGH-WATER size for the life of the thread. jrt shrinks its
           own heap buffer back to 64 KiB after every read; the direct buffer the
           JDK borrowed to fill it is never shrunk. So one 64 MiB packet pins
           64 MiB per thread that touched it, permanently. That is why the pool
           refills to >=95% within seconds of every restart, and why raising the
           ceiling ALONE would only move the wall further out.

           Hence both flags:
             * jdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize caps what may be RETAINED — anything
               larger is allocated and freed per read instead of cached. 256 KiB
               is 4x jrt's own maxInputBufferSize (64 KiB) and 16x its READ_SIZE
               (16 KiB), so the steady-state path still hits the cache and pays
               no extra allocation; only the outliers that cause this stop being
               kept.
             * MaxDirectMemorySize is headroom for the transient peak that
               remains, since a large read still allocates its full size for the
               duration of the read. It is a CEILING, not a reservation — the JVM
               allocates nothing up front — which is what makes 512m safe to ship
               in a package that also deploys onto laptops and testcontainers.

           NOT touched: the heap. It was never the constraint here, and unlike
           the ceiling above it IS reserved (-Xms1536m), so raising it would tax
           every small deployment for a fault none of them had. Size it per
           deployment with <jvm allocated-memory="N%" /> instead.

           NOT removed: -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError. It is what turned a
           per-connection shortfall into a total outage, but the fix belongs at
           the trigger rather than the response — a HEAP OOM leaves the JVM in an
           undefined state, and a container that keeps serving after one is worse
           than one the sentinel restarts. These options are appended after
           Vespa's derived flags and win over them (Vespa's own tuning guide sets
           -Xmx through this attribute), so an operator who disagrees can append
           -XX:-ExitOnOutOfMemoryError here. ContainerJvmIT reads the running
           JVM's command line and asserts these actually took effect — a silent
           no-op here looks exactly like a fix. -->
      <jvm options="-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=512m -Djdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize=262144" />
      <node hostalias="node1" />
    </nodes>
  </container>

  <content id="content" version="1.0">
    <redundancy>1</redundancy>

    <documents garbage-collection="true">
      <!-- NIP-40 expiry as a SERVER-SIDE constraint: the engine periodically
           (default hourly) removes every event whose expires_at has passed —
           the closest Vespa has to a SQLite trigger. expires_at is ALWAYS
           written (Long.MAX_VALUE = never expires), so the selection keeps
           every non-expiring doc. The store's own guards stay: the read-time
           `expires_at > now` filter (expiry must be exact, GC is periodic)
           and the insert-time block on already-expired events (which needs no
           round trip — the expiration tag is on the event itself). -->
      <document type="event" mode="index" selection="event.expires_at &gt; now()" />
      <!-- Ranking state per pubkey; GLOBAL so event docs can reference it and
           import the trust tensors into their own ranking (parent/child). -->
      <document type="reputation" mode="index" global="true" />
    </documents>

    <nodes>
      <node distribution-key="0" hostalias="node1" />
    </nodes>

    <!-- Single-node dev tuning: Vespa blocks feeds at 80% disk / 85% memory
         by default, which thin-provisioned or small dev disks trip
         immediately (statvfs reports the host, not the workload). Raised for
         the local engine; a real multi-node deployment should size disks
         instead of leaning on this. -->
    <tuning>
      <resource-limits>
        <disk>0.985</disk>
        <memory>0.92</memory>
      </resource-limits>
    </tuning>

    <!-- Feed threads get 40% of the cores, NOT all of them — measured on the
         mixed-load A/B (2 writers x 16 readers, 2M-doc corpus, warm engine,
         4 cores), 0.4 beat 1.0 on BOTH sides:

           write-only  444 -> 753 ev/s   (+70%)
           mixed       385 -> 652 ev/s and 89 -> 112 q/s, p99 677 -> 552ms

         The store's write path is itself READ-heavy (dedup preloads, guard
         probes, supersession searches between the puts), so at 1.0 the feed
         pipeline starves the very reads its own batches wait on — full-core
         feeding throttled ingest, not just queries. Raw index-level feeding
         (no store semantics) may still prefer more; re-measure with
         BENCH_MIXED before changing. -->
    <config name="vespa.config.search.core.proton">
      <feeding>
        <concurrency>0.4</concurrency>
      </feeding>
      <!-- One matching thread per query. A relay's load is MANY concurrent
           queries, not one big query needing low latency; splitting a query
           across cores would cut its latency but waste cores that another query
           could be using, lowering total throughput. With 1 thread/query the
           cores stay saturated with independent queries — the throughput-optimal
           setting for concurrent serving (a latency-critical deployment would
           raise it). -->
      <numthreadspersearch>1</numthreadspersearch>
      <!-- The transaction log is a durability journal that is pruned once its
           contents are flushed to the persisted index. Its default flush
           trigger (maxtlssize) is 20 GiB, and for small events the in-memory
           index never hits its own memory trigger first — so the translog was
           the dominant trigger and grew to ~19 GiB, filling the disk and
           feed-blocking the engine mid-sync. Cap it at 4 GiB so it flushes and
           prunes far sooner; the memory trigger stays default. -->
      <flush>
        <memory>
          <maxtlssize>4294967296</maxtlssize>
        </memory>
      </flush>
    </config>
  </content>

</services>
